# sdkconfig overlay applied ONLY to -DTR_BOARD_V9=1 and -DTR_BOARD_M1=1 builds,
# on top of sdkconfig.defaults. Selected by the project CMakeLists, not by the
# build-dir name — see there.
#
# Exists because PSRAM presence is a board fact and sdkconfig is per-project.
# V7/V8 have no PSRAM part (the MRAM is what they use), so compiling PSRAM
# support into their images would buy a failed init and a scary boot warning on
# a board that is working correctly. PSRAM is ALL this file sets: the five
# CONFIG_SPIRAM lines at the bottom. M1 (rocket-computer-mini's out computer,
# the same ESP32-S3RH2 with the same in-package PSRAM) shares it for those
# lines; the V9 board history in the prose is rocket-computer's, not the mini's.

# === Boot NOR: no longer declared here (#916, owner decision 2026-09-09) ===
# Until #1314 this file also carried CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_16MB=y, because
# U13 (the S3's boot flash) is a 16 MB GD25Q128ESIG on V9/V10 where everything
# fabbed before the 2026-08-09 close-out carries an 8 MB W25Q64JVXGIQ:
# hardware/rocket-computer/bom.csv has the swap at 8f87aa0, and the V7 board
# in hand measures ef/7017 = 8 MB on both MCUs (2026-08-24).
#
# That per-board declaration is gone. Every project now declares the SMALLEST
# standard size that fits its own partition table, not the part fitted, and
# tinkerrocket-idf/tools/check_flash_declarations.py fails the Docs job on any
# headroom above the table — including a 16 MB line put back here — and on the
# mini's two processors disagreeing about their shared board's part (#1310).
# partitions.csv ends at 0x624000 (6.14 MB), so the 8 MB in sdkconfig.defaults
# covers every board this project builds for (V7, V8, V9, M1), and a V9 or M1
# build generates CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE="8MB".
#
# What that costs a V9 is deliberate: it logs "Detected size(16384k) larger
# than the size in the binary image header(8192k)" on every boot and the top
# half of the part is unaddressable. Under-declaring costs only that;
# over-declaring is the direction that boot-loops an 8 MB V7/V8 inside the
# bootloader, with no firmware log at all. If a partition ever crosses 8 MB,
# grow the table first and then raise the base declaration to the next
# standard size — that is the workflow the guard enforces, not a bypass.
# PSRAM below is the setting that IS still a board fact, and still lives here.

# === PSRAM: the log ring's real home on V9/V10 (#822) ===
# U15 is an ESP32-S3RH2 with 2 MB of in-package QUAD PSRAM. That part was not
# chosen by accident — deleting the MRAM (U12) and moving to the embedded-PSRAM
# variant was one decision (prefab review I18), and the PSRAM is what buffers
# NAND writes now. Leaving CONFIG_SPIRAM unset meant the ring fell back to
# 64 KB of internal RAM and the replacement sat idle.
#
# The rail objection is settled, not waived: prior finding H7 (log flash sharing
# the S3's internal ~14 ohm R_SPI with the PSRAM) was FIXED before fab — U13's
# VCC moved to +3V3 and OUT_VDD_SPI now carries only U15.29, verified against
# the live netlist. Budget is ~30 mA x 14 = 0.42 V -> ~2.88 V against a 2.7 V
# minimum. Note this is the OPPOSITE of the mini, whose sdkconfig says "PSRAM:
# deliberately OFF" precisely because that board still shares the rail.
#
# One residual, WORKLIST M-4, and it is SMALLER than that entry claims: C27 is
# 1 uF in the fabbed V9 (close-out 8f87aa0, 2026-08-09) — WORKLIST still says
# 10 uF because it was written from the 2026-08-05 review and never updated
# after the resize landed. So tau is ~14 us, not ~140 us, and VDD_SPI ramp is
# NOT a suspect for flaky PSRAM bring-up. What remains is only the missing
# 0.1 uF (the HDG asks for 0.1 + 1 uF); there is no pad for it, so it is a
# next-revision item, not an assembly one. PSRAM init is announced in the boot
# log either way.
CONFIG_SPIRAM=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_MODE_QUAD=y
# 40 MHz is the IDF default and far more than enough — the ring takes ~156 KB/s
# against tens of MB/s available. Buys timing margin we have no reason to spend.
CONFIG_SPIRAM_SPEED_40M=y
# CAPS_ALLOC, *not* the default MALLOC. Only code that explicitly asks for
# MALLOC_CAP_SPIRAM lands in PSRAM; plain malloc() keeps returning internal RAM
# exactly as before. That keeps this change surgical — the log ring moves, and
# nothing else in the firmware silently relocates into slower, non-DMA memory.
CONFIG_SPIRAM_USE_CAPS_ALLOC=y
# Boot without PSRAM instead of panicking. Written when these lines lived in
# sdkconfig.defaults and reached the V7/V8 images too; since the #822 split
# only V9 and M1 builds see this file, so that case is gone. It stays because
# the failure it covers is still real: a PSRAM that fails to initialise, or a
# V9 image reaching a board without one over USB (the #1125 suffix check runs
# only on OTA), should boot to a log rather than abort before app_main.
# TR_LogToFlash falls back to the internal ring on its own.
CONFIG_SPIRAM_IGNORE_NOTFOUND=y

